1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,971
A nightly evaluation job has
been green for three weeks.

2
00:00:08,011 --> 00:00:12,986
Someone opens the saved report by chance
and finds failing tests in it, from the

3
00:00:13,026 --> 00:00:18,190
first of those nights on. The step ran the
tests and piped their output into tee, a

4
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:22,955
program that copies what it receives
into a file, to keep a copy of the log.

5
00:00:22,995 --> 00:00:27,406
A pipe is the vertical bar that feeds
one command's output to the next.

6
00:00:27,446 --> 00:00:30,666
The tests failed. tee
succeeded. The step was green.

7
00:00:31,006 --> 00:00:35,840
Nobody wrote a wrong command. The command
was run by a shell, the program that reads

8
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:39,205
and runs command lines, with
options nobody had looked up.

9
00:00:39,245 --> 00:00:43,789
In this video you look them up, and you
reproduce the green failure on your own

10
00:00:43,829 --> 00:00:46,748
machine in two lines. Keep
that green step in mind.

11
00:00:48,588 --> 00:00:52,557
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:52,597 --> 00:00:57,947
You know from video 142 that a step is the
unit of process: each run step is a new

13
00:00:57,987 --> 00:01:02,877
shell process. This video takes that
sentence seriously, in two directions.

14
00:01:03,217 --> 00:01:08,615
First: which shell, started how? The runner
uses a documented command template, and

15
00:01:08,655 --> 00:01:13,267
the template differs depending on
whether you wrote a shell key or not.

16
00:01:13,307 --> 00:01:18,443
That difference decides whether a failing
command in a pipeline fails the step.

17
00:01:18,783 --> 00:01:23,488
Second: if every step is a new process,
how does anything get from one step to the

18
00:01:23,528 --> 00:01:28,059
next, and from one job to the next? Through
files that the runner provides, and

19
00:01:28,099 --> 00:01:29,737
through declared job outputs.

20
00:01:30,077 --> 00:01:34,158
The demonstration reproduces the
runner's shell invocation locally.

21
00:01:34,198 --> 00:01:37,479
It's the same bash with the
same options, on your Mac.

22
00:01:37,519 --> 00:01:38,893
Nothing runs on GitHub.

23
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:42,312
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:42,652 --> 00:01:44,064
After this video you can:

25
00:01:44,404 --> 00:01:48,441
state the default shell and its options
for a run step on a Linux runner;

26
00:01:48,781 --> 00:01:53,666
explain why a pipeline with a failing first
command can leave a step green, and fix

27
00:01:53,706 --> 00:01:53,844
it;

28
00:01:54,184 --> 00:01:57,949
explain why a variable set in
one step is gone in the next;

29
00:01:58,289 --> 00:02:03,005
pass a value to a later step and to a
later job through GITHUB_OUTPUT and job

30
00:02:03,045 --> 00:02:03,499
outputs;

31
00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:06,768
recognize the deprecated
command the section names.

32
00:02:08,608 --> 00:02:13,725
In one sentence. Every run step is a new
shell process started from a documented

33
00:02:13,765 --> 00:02:19,140
command template, so state survives a step
only if it's written to a file the runner

34
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:24,297
provides: GITHUB_OUTPUT for named outputs,
GITHUB_ENV for environment variables,

35
00:02:24,337 --> 00:02:26,425
GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY for a report.

36
00:02:26,765 --> 00:02:31,634
The shell, precisely. The runner writes your
run text to a temporary script file and

37
00:02:31,674 --> 00:02:35,842
executes it with a template in which
a placeholder stands for that file.

38
00:02:36,182 --> 00:02:41,227
If you write nothing, on Linux or macOS,
the runner executes bash -e with the file.

39
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:47,122
If you write shell: bash, on any platform,
it executes bash --noprofile --norc -eo

40
00:02:47,162 --> 00:02:48,691
pipefail with the file.

41
00:02:49,031 --> 00:02:51,617
If you write nothing on
Windows: PowerShell Core.

42
00:02:51,957 --> 00:02:56,482
If you write nothing inside a job
container on Linux: sh, not bash.

43
00:02:56,822 --> 00:02:59,422
And shell: python runs
the file with Python.

44
00:02:59,762 --> 00:03:05,161
Quick quiz, with the table still in front
of you. On a Linux runner, bash already

45
00:03:05,201 --> 00:03:09,997
runs your script. You add shell: bash
to the step. Does anything change?

46
00:03:10,037 --> 00:03:13,220
A, nothing. B, the shell's
options. Your answer?

47
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:23,540
B, the options change. Look at the first
two rows again, because they're the hook.

48
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,435
Both have -e. Only the
second has pipefail.

49
00:03:26,775 --> 00:03:31,900
-e stops the script at the first command
that fails, that is, a command that returns

50
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:35,958
an exit status other than zero. It
doesn't look inside a pipeline.

51
00:03:35,998 --> 00:03:41,061
The status of a pipeline is the status of
its last command, unless pipefail is set.

52
00:03:41,101 --> 00:03:45,488
So the implicit default lets a failing
test command piped into tee pass.

53
00:03:45,828 --> 00:03:50,561
The irony is worth saying aloud: writing
shell: bash explicitly, which looks like

54
00:03:50,601 --> 00:03:54,333
stating the default, changes the
behavior. It isn't the default.

55
00:03:54,373 --> 00:03:57,574
The default is a weaker
invocation of the same program.

56
00:03:57,914 --> 00:04:03,304
Workflow 3 of the course sets defaults.run.shell:
bash at the top for this reason.

57
00:04:03,344 --> 00:04:07,674
One line, and every run step in
the file gets the strict template.

58
00:04:08,014 --> 00:04:13,081
The files, precisely. The runner creates
files and puts their paths in environment

59
00:04:13,121 --> 00:04:13,704
variables.

60
00:04:14,044 --> 00:04:19,444
GITHUB_OUTPUT: you append name=value.
The step, which needs an id, then has an

61
00:04:19,484 --> 00:04:25,303
output of that name under the steps context.
Job outputs are limited to 1 MB per job

62
00:04:25,343 --> 00:04:26,558
and 50 MB per run.

63
00:04:26,898 --> 00:04:31,883
GITHUB_ENV: you append NAME=value. Later
steps of the job get the environment

64
00:04:31,923 --> 00:04:37,039
variable. The writing step itself doesn't.
And it can't override variables that

65
00:04:37,079 --> 00:04:38,997
start with GITHUB_ or RUNNER_.

66
00:04:39,337 --> 00:04:43,671
GITHUB_PATH: you append a directory, and
it's prepended to PATH for later steps.

67
00:04:44,011 --> 00:04:49,522
GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: you append Markdown,
and it's shown on the run's summary page.

68
00:04:49,562 --> 00:04:54,137
The limit is 1 MiB per step, and 20
step summaries are shown per job.

69
00:04:54,477 --> 00:04:58,153
Crossing a job boundary. A step
output is local to its job.

70
00:04:58,193 --> 00:05:03,255
To cross to another job it must be re-exported
as a job output, under the outputs

71
00:05:03,295 --> 00:05:07,538
key of the job, and read in the
other job through the needs context.

72
00:05:07,578 --> 00:05:12,199
Two details: an output that looks like
a secret is dropped with a message.

73
00:05:12,239 --> 00:05:14,907
And multi-line values
use a delimiter form.

74
00:05:15,247 --> 00:05:20,590
What doesn't cross a job boundary this
way: files. Job outputs are strings.

75
00:05:20,630 --> 00:05:23,820
Files cross as artifacts,
which is video 146.

76
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:28,656
Older tutorials write outputs with a
line that starts with two colons and

77
00:05:28,696 --> 00:05:33,813
set-output. That form, and save-state, are
deprecated since the eleventh of October

78
00:05:33,853 --> 00:05:38,660
2022. They still work with a warning.
The planned removal was postponed on the

79
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:43,382
twenty-fourth of July 2023, and the
current workflow commands page no longer

80
00:05:43,422 --> 00:05:48,415
documents them. The reason for the change
is visible in the form itself: it was a

81
00:05:48,455 --> 00:05:53,634
magic line on standard output. So any program
whose output a step printed, including

82
00:05:53,674 --> 00:05:56,990
a test that echoed untrusted
input, could set outputs.

83
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:02,234
Think of a job as a row of
sealed rooms along one corridor.

84
00:06:02,574 --> 00:06:07,261
Each run step is a room. A new shell is
started in it, your script runs, the shell

85
00:06:07,301 --> 00:06:11,815
exits, and the room is cleared: its
variables, its current directory, its shell

86
00:06:11,855 --> 00:06:15,907
options. What's left is the corridor,
which is the runner's filesystem.

87
00:06:15,947 --> 00:06:19,250
Files you wrote to disk are
still there for the next step.

88
00:06:19,590 --> 00:06:25,223
Underneath the rooms runs a pneumatic tube:
a small file whose path the runner gives

89
00:06:25,263 --> 00:06:30,017
to every step. A step may drop notes
into it, one per line, name=value.

90
00:06:30,057 --> 00:06:35,419
When the step ends, the runner reads the
notes and makes them available to later

91
00:06:35,459 --> 00:06:36,364
steps by name.

92
00:06:36,704 --> 00:06:39,922
Where the picture breaks: the
corridor ends at the job.

93
00:06:39,962 --> 00:06:44,720
The next job is in another building, on
another machine, and neither the corridor

94
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:49,578
nor the tube reaches it. Only what the job
declares at its door, under outputs, is

95
00:06:49,618 --> 00:06:51,651
carried over, and only as a string.

96
00:06:51,991 --> 00:06:55,531
The second break concerns the
rooms. They aren't all built alike.

97
00:06:55,571 --> 00:06:59,882
Whether a room has the pipefail rule
depends on a key you may not have written.

98
00:07:01,722 --> 00:07:06,206
Three mechanisms on one picture.
Nothing in the shell survives a step.

99
00:07:06,246 --> 00:07:10,989
A named value survives through the
file. Only a job output leaves the box.

100
00:07:12,829 --> 00:07:15,537
Step 1: the same script, two templates.

101
00:07:15,877 --> 00:07:19,934
The script has three lines. set
-u. Then false piped into tee.

102
00:07:19,974 --> 00:07:23,502
Then an echo that says the
step reached its last line.

103
00:07:23,542 --> 00:07:28,920
false always fails. Under the first template,
does the script reach its last line,

104
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:33,282
and what's the exit status? And
under the second? Say it out loud.

105
00:07:38,122 --> 00:07:42,091
Under bash -e, the last line is
printed and the exit status is 0.

106
00:07:42,131 --> 00:07:46,409
The step would be green, although
false failed, because tee succeeded.

107
00:07:46,449 --> 00:07:51,529
There's the green failure from the opening.
Under the shell: bash template the same

108
00:07:51,569 --> 00:07:54,798
script stops with status 1
and never prints the line.

109
00:07:55,138 --> 00:07:59,766
This answers the CTO's fourth question.
A green job proves only that the last

110
00:07:59,806 --> 00:08:03,828
command of every pipeline succeeded,
unless the shell was declared.

111
00:08:04,168 --> 00:08:10,472
Try it now, thirty seconds, in your own
terminal. Type false | tee /dev/null, press

112
00:08:10,512 --> 00:08:14,600
Enter, then type echo $?.
Nothing is written anywhere.

113
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:16,434
Which number do you get?

114
00:08:18,774 --> 00:08:24,079
Zero. echo $? prints the exit status of
the line before it, and a pipeline reports

115
00:08:24,119 --> 00:08:28,316
its last command. tee succeeded,
so the failure of false is gone.

116
00:08:28,656 --> 00:08:30,997
Step 2: a new process per step.

117
00:08:31,337 --> 00:08:36,224
The first script exports BUILD_ID, changes
into build, and prints the directory.

118
00:08:36,264 --> 00:08:41,152
The second prints BUILD_ID and the directory.
What does the second script print?

119
00:08:41,192 --> 00:08:42,446
Make your prediction.

120
00:08:47,286 --> 00:08:51,733
BUILD_ID is empty, and the directory
is the starting directory again.

121
00:08:51,773 --> 00:08:56,026
The export and the cd of the first
script didn't reach the second.

122
00:08:56,066 --> 00:08:59,538
On a runner the same happens
between steps. That's why

123
00:08:59,578 --> 00:09:02,919
defaults.run.working-directory
and GITHUB_ENV exist.

124
00:09:03,259 --> 00:09:04,995
Step 3: GITHUB_OUTPUT.

125
00:09:05,335 --> 00:09:10,737
On a runner the path is given to each step.
Here a file in the sandbox stands in for

126
00:09:10,777 --> 00:09:10,932
it.

127
00:09:11,272 --> 00:09:15,817
The file is an append-only list of
assignments. Two single-line values first.

128
00:09:15,857 --> 00:09:20,699
Then a multi-line value in the delimiter
form: the name, two less-than signs and a

129
00:09:20,739 --> 00:09:24,034
delimiter word, the lines,
and the delimiter word again.

130
00:09:24,374 --> 00:09:28,297
Two habits, both from the textbook.
Always quote the variable.

131
00:09:28,337 --> 00:09:33,538
And always use two greater-than signs: a
single one discards what earlier lines of

132
00:09:33,578 --> 00:09:34,816
the same step wrote.

133
00:09:35,156 --> 00:09:37,289
Step 4: the deprecated form.

134
00:09:37,629 --> 00:09:41,620
It's an echo. The "command" is
whatever appears on standard output.

135
00:09:41,660 --> 00:09:46,495
Any program that prints that line sets an
output. When you see it in an inherited

136
00:09:46,535 --> 00:09:47,758
workflow, replace it.

137
00:09:48,098 --> 00:09:53,389
Read the file in the order you know. Trigger:
pushes to main, tags that start with

138
00:09:53,429 --> 00:09:58,135
v, and manual runs. Permissions: read.
Then the line this video is about:

139
00:09:58,175 --> 00:09:59,761
defaults.run.shell: bash.

140
00:10:00,101 --> 00:10:05,238
In the build job, the step with the ID
describe appends one line to GITHUB_OUTPUT.

141
00:10:05,278 --> 00:10:08,774
The job declares two outputs
that refer to step outputs.

142
00:10:08,814 --> 00:10:13,889
The report job says needs: build, runs on
a new machine, maps the two job outputs

143
00:10:13,929 --> 00:10:19,003
into env, and writes them into the step
summary. The comment in the file says it:

144
00:10:19,043 --> 00:10:23,045
nothing from the build job is here
except what needs hands over.

145
00:10:23,385 --> 00:10:27,145
Now Lab 26.3 on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

146
00:10:27,185 --> 00:10:32,152
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference,

147
00:10:32,192 --> 00:10:37,098
and no GitHub output was captured by the
authors. After the merge to main starts a

148
00:10:37,138 --> 00:10:40,778
run, open the run, open the build
job, and expand a run step.

149
00:10:40,818 --> 00:10:45,483
Look in the step's log for where it states
the shell that ran your script, and

150
00:10:45,523 --> 00:10:47,594
compare it with the template table.

151
00:10:47,934 --> 00:10:52,431
The textbook doesn't describe that part of
the log, so treat what you find as your

152
00:10:52,471 --> 00:10:55,861
own observation and check it
against the documented templates.

153
00:10:55,901 --> 00:11:00,398
Then open the run's summary page and find
the two values that crossed from one job

154
00:11:00,438 --> 00:11:01,118
to the other.

155
00:11:02,958 --> 00:11:04,606
Five mistakes to watch for.

156
00:11:04,946 --> 00:11:08,849
Piping a test command into tee
in a step without a shell key.

157
00:11:08,889 --> 00:11:14,019
Root cause: the implicit template is bash
-e without pipefail, so the pipeline's

158
00:11:14,059 --> 00:11:15,441
status is that of tee.

159
00:11:15,781 --> 00:11:20,860
Setting a variable or changing directory
in one step and relying on it in the next.

160
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:23,882
Root cause: each run step
is a new shell process.

161
00:11:24,222 --> 00:11:29,535
Writing to GITHUB_OUTPUT with a single >.
Root cause: that truncates the file and

162
00:11:29,575 --> 00:11:32,906
discards what earlier lines
of the same step wrote.

163
00:11:33,246 --> 00:11:38,511
Reading a step's output from another job.
Root cause: a step output is local to its

164
00:11:38,551 --> 00:11:42,794
job; it must be re-exported as a
job output and read through needs.

165
00:11:43,134 --> 00:11:47,881
Assuming bash inside a job container. Root
cause: the default shell there is sh.

166
00:11:49,721 --> 00:11:54,820
Now, out of the lab. The nightly evaluation
from the hook, on an AI and ML team.

167
00:11:54,860 --> 00:11:59,830
The step is one line: run the evaluation,
pipe the output into tee to keep the

168
00:11:59,870 --> 00:12:04,905
report as a file, and upload the file
afterwards. The workflow has no shell key

169
00:12:04,945 --> 00:12:05,483
anywhere.

170
00:12:05,823 --> 00:12:09,816
The diagnosis follows the reading
order. What ran the script?

171
00:12:09,856 --> 00:12:14,840
bash -e and the file. What's the status
of a pipeline under that invocation?

172
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:18,410
The status of the last command.
Which command is last?

173
00:12:18,450 --> 00:12:18,675
tee.

174
00:12:19,015 --> 00:12:23,799
There are two repairs, and they're the two
ways to get the strict template or its

175
00:12:23,839 --> 00:12:28,802
effect: declare shell: bash for the step or
as a default for the file, or stop using

176
00:12:28,842 --> 00:12:33,567
a pipeline for the command whose status
matters. The prevention is a line in the

177
00:12:33,607 --> 00:12:38,272
team's workflow template: defaults.run.shell:
bash at the top of every file, as

178
00:12:38,312 --> 00:12:39,344
workflow 3 has it.

179
00:12:41,184 --> 00:12:47,434
Your turn. Do Exercise 26.5,
"Two shells, three scripts", in

180
00:12:47,474 --> 00:12:51,418
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

181
00:12:51,758 --> 00:12:56,502
For each script and each shell, predict
the exit status and the last line printed

182
00:12:56,542 --> 00:13:01,345
before you check anything. Write the full
command line the runner would use beside

183
00:13:01,385 --> 00:13:02,290
each prediction.

184
00:13:02,630 --> 00:13:07,871
The challenge is Exercise 28.2, "The nightly
evaluation that never fails", in the

185
00:13:07,911 --> 00:13:08,523
same file.

186
00:13:12,363 --> 00:13:16,949
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
quotes the template, option by option, and

187
00:13:16,989 --> 00:13:21,689
says which option is missing and what that
option does to the status of a pipeline.

188
00:13:21,729 --> 00:13:26,315
It gives two repairs that are different
in kind, and says which one it would make

189
00:13:26,355 --> 00:13:31,055
the team default. The follow-up moves the
step into a job that runs in a container:

190
00:13:31,095 --> 00:13:35,738
recall what the default shell is there,
and reason about what that does to both of

191
00:13:35,778 --> 00:13:36,480
your repairs.

192
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:43,476
Let's land this. The green job from the
opening, in one line: without pipefail, the

193
00:13:43,516 --> 00:13:45,479
step reported the status of tee.

194
00:13:45,819 --> 00:13:47,478
You should now be able to say:

195
00:13:47,818 --> 00:13:54,197
Without a shell key on Linux the runner
executes bash -e; with shell: bash it

196
00:13:54,237 --> 00:13:58,032
executes bash --noprofile
--norc -eo pipefail.

197
00:13:58,372 --> 00:14:03,109
Without pipefail, a pipeline's status is
its last command's, so a failing command

198
00:14:03,149 --> 00:14:05,291
piped into tee leaves the step green.

199
00:14:05,631 --> 00:14:09,973
Each run step is a new process;
exports and cd do not survive it.

200
00:14:10,313 --> 00:14:14,708
A value reaches a later step through
GITHUB_OUTPUT and the steps context, and a

201
00:14:14,748 --> 00:14:17,515
later job through a declared
job output and needs.

202
00:14:17,855 --> 00:14:21,372
The set-output form is deprecated;
replace it when you see it.

203
00:14:23,212 --> 00:14:26,315
Read section 20A.7 of Chapter 20A.

204
00:14:26,655 --> 00:14:31,649
Today you reproduced a green failure in two
lines, and you can carry a value across

205
00:14:31,689 --> 00:14:36,562
a step and across a job. Before the next
video, look for a pipe in a workflow you

206
00:14:36,602 --> 00:14:41,172
know, and check for a shell key. Next
time: what the checkout action does by

207
00:14:41,212 --> 00:14:46,267
default. One commit, no tags, and the merge
ref. Until then, look at the state first

208
00:14:46,307 --> 00:14:48,753
and type second. See you in the next one.
